        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(gateway): split websocket message handler",
        "body": "Closes #105962\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe gateway WebSocket message handler had grown to 2,866 lines, concentrating every connection phase and authenticated request path in one file. That made the product's busiest transport path difficult to review, test, and change safely.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSplit the handler at existing lifecycle seams: frame admission, protocol/origin/authentication, device proof and pairing, node/session attachment, hello finalization, and authenticated request dispatch. The entrypoint remains the lifecycle orchestrator; shared mutable connection state, auth ordering, flood guards, credential-mutation barriers, payload limits, cleanup claims, and load-bearing invariant comments are preserved. No gateway protocol or user-visible behavior changes.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change. Maintainers get focused gateway modules, all below the 500-line LOC ratchet, with the existing end-to-end handler tests continuing to exercise the public entrypoint.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox: `corepack pnpm tsgo --pretty false` — passed.\n- Blacksmith Testbox: `corepack pnpm test src/gateway/server/ws-connection` — 40 files, 500 tests passed.\n- Blacksmith Testbox: `corepack pnpm build` — passed.\n- Broader gateway test set: every reported assertion passed in both parallel and serial runs; Vitest then ended with the same `Worker exited unexpectedly` harness failure after completion.\n- Exact-head `check:changed` reached the LOC gate with no refactor violation; it stopped on unrelated LOC-baseline drift already present on current `main`.\n- Autoreview: authentication, pairing/device authorization, request dispatch/types, and node/session/hello slices all clean with no accepted findings.\n- `git diff --check` — passed.\n\nAI-assisted: yes. Generated/refactored with Codex; reviewed and remotely validated as described above. No transcript attached because transcript publication consent was not provided.\n",
